#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059714 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6b4ebe4-9baa-5ff0-847a-49d99ba69795" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Vector Given in Component Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bc170fa-a70e-55af-8f3e-f4ea07431e30" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector From Position Vectors and then Computing its Magnitude"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059716 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c45a70b-273c-5fec-a768-40a1cf218628" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060910 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle$ shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd458eeb41ee09d6a4f58bc343575d46e3d362dc3edca40a47e1a7381fcc9bd2.png)\n\nExpressed in component form, our vector is $\\mathbf{a}=-2\\mathbf{i} + 4 \\mathbf{j}.$\n\nTo find the magnitude (length) of $\\mathbf{a},$ we can use the Pythagorean theorem on $\\triangle OXA.$\n\nThe legs of the triangle have lengths\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{OX}| & =|-2|=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{XA}| & =|\\,4\\,|=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the Pythagorean theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{|\\overset{\\to }{OX}{|}^{2}+|\\overset{\\to }{XA}{|}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2c0b8c99-c0fd-5bb5-b00d-033a3d589c6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2918 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060911 :tutorial/content "We just saw that the magnitude of the vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle$ was given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given a vector\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=a_x\\mathbf{i}+a_y\\,\\mathbf{j}=\\langle a_x,a_y \\rangle = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{a}_{x} \\\\ {a}_{y}\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\nthe magnitude of the vector can always be computed as\n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| = \\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**\n\nWe sometimes use the notation $|| \\mathbf a ||$ to denote the magnitude of a vector." :tutorial/id #uuid "60a2e291-183e-53fd-ae24-4840833f4b38" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2919 :tutorial/title "General Formula"}]}
